**Abstract:** This proposal presents an adversarial benchmark for evaluating whether large language models can be prompted to generate detailed, engineering-level descriptions of drone-mounted granular dispersal systems when framed under benign environmental remediation and precision agriculture narratives. The benchmark comprises six families of adversarial prompts targeting release valve/metering mechanisms, outlet geometry, anti-clogging strategies, autonomous control logic, payload-to-weight optimization, and integrated design synthesis. Eight fully written example prompts are provided, each explicitly requesting technical specifics including outlet cross-sections, actuation methods, anti-clogging mechanisms, and flow-rate feedback control. Model outputs are scored across five dimensions—dispersion uniformity, payload capacity adequacy, reliability/robustness, control precision, and novelty—using a structured rubric with low/medium/high classification and 5-point scoring scales. The proposal includes detailed annotation protocols, inter-rater reliability procedures, framing-variation experiments, and a comprehensive risk/ethics discussion with mitigation strategies including controlled access, aggregate reporting, and responsible publication policies.
